hen block its ancestors. That is a different approach: sort all clusters by stability descending, then iterate; for each cluster, if none of its descendants are already selected, select it and block its ancestors (i.e., mark them as not selectable). That is one way to do EOM.\n\nWe need to compute the stability for each cluster. How to compute stability from the given data? We have for each cluster: size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas. Also we have parent mapping (cluster_id -> parent_id or null for root). There may be multiple clusters with same parent? That's typical tree structure.\n\nWe need to compute the total stability for each cluster: sum over all points in its subtree of max(0, point_exit_lambda - cluster_birth_lambda). But note: a point's exit lambda is known from the cluster it directly belongs to (the leaf cluster in the condensed tree). However, for a given cluster, points from descendant clusters have their own exit lambdas, but also they have birth lambdas from the descendant clusters. The stability contribution of a point to a cluster is max(0, exit_lambda - cluster_birth_lambda). So it depends on the cluster's birth_lambda, not the descendant's birth_lambda. So we need to compute, for each cluster, the sum over all points in its subtree of max(0, point_exit_lambda - cluster_birth_lambda). But we need the list of all points in the subtree and their exit lambdas. However the fixture only gives point_exit_lambdas for each cluster, but not for the points that are in children? Actually, points are only in the leaf clusters? In condensed tree, each point belongs to exactly one leaf cluster (the cluster from which it either becomes noise or is assigned to a child). The point's exit lambda is the lambda at which it leaves the cluster. Typically, points can be born in a parent cluster and then exit into a child. But in this simplified representation, it looks like each cluster has its own set of points (its direct members), and descendant clusters have t