(and cluster ID for ties), iterate; for each, if none of its descendants have been selected yet, select it and mark all its ancestors as blocked (i.e., not selectable). So we need to implement that.\n\nWe also need to compute stability for each cluster as described: sum over its own points (point_exit_lambdas) of max(0, exit - birth). But we need to verify from the fixture: For cluster c0: birth_lambda=0.2, point_exit_lambdas=[0.4,0.6,0.9]. Then contributions: 0.4-0.2=0.2; 0.6-0.2=0.4; 0.9-0.2=0.7; sum=1.3. For c1: birth=0.5, exits=[0.7,1.0,1.2] -> 0.2,0.5,0.7 sum=1.4. For c2: birth=0.8, exits=[1.1] -> 0.3 sum=0.3. min_cluster_size=3. Drop clusters smaller than 3: c2 size=2 => drop. c0 and c1 size>=3 retained. So retained clusters: c0 (stability 1.3), c1 (stability 1.4). Now rank by decreasing stability: c1 (1.4) first, then c0 (1.3). Check descendants of c1: none? Parent is c0, but descendants? c1 has no children? It might have none based on parent map? Actually parent map: c0:null, c1:c0, c2:c0. So c1 has no children. So no selected descendant blocks c1 -> select c1, then block its ancestors: c0. Then next cluster c0: it has descendant c1 already selected? Yes, c1 is descendant of c0. So c0 cannot be selected because a descendant is already selected. So selected IDs = [\"c1\"]. Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs. That seems plausible.\n\nNow we must implement `solve(case)` that given the case payload (as dict) does the above.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Validate input: check that case is a dict, has \"min_cluster_size\", \"clusters\", \"parent\". clusters must be a list of dicts each with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas (list of floats). parent is dict mapping cluster_id to parent_id or None. Ensure no cycles, all cluster_ids present, etc. Raise ValueError if any issue.\n\n2. Build tree structure: for each cluster, store its attributes. Also compute descendants? We'll need to know descendants for selection blocking. We can build adjacency list of children from parent mapping.\n\n3. Filter clusters: keep only those where size >= min_cluster_size.\n\n4. For each retained cluster, compute stability = sum(max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for exit_lambda in point_exit_lambdas). Note: even for dropped clusters, we don't need stability.\n\n5. Sort retained clusters by ( -stability, cluster_id ) for primary key. In case of tie stability, cluster_id ascending.\n\n6. Now selection: maintain a set of selected cluster_ids, and a set of blocked cluster_ids (ancestors of selected ones). Also need to know descendants for each cluster to check if any descendant is selected. We can precompute for each cluster the set of all descendant cluster_ids (including itself? No, descendant means strictly lower). Also ancestors chain.\n\n7. Iterate over sorted list: for each cluster, if any of its descendants (strict) is already selected, skip. Else, select it, then mark all ancestors as blocked (adding to set). Also update selected set.\n\n8. Finally, build result: For each retained cluster (including those not selected?), the problem says \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" That implies we need to output a list of stability rows (maybe for all retained clusters?) and the list of selected cluster IDs sorted. What exactly are \"retained stability rows\"? Likely for each retained cluster, we need to output something like { cluster_id, stability } and then selected_ids sorted. The description: \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So we should output a dict with two keys: \"stability_rows\" (list of objects with cluster_id and stability, sorted by cluster_id? Or unsorted? Probably sorted by cluster_id). And \"selected_ids\" (list of cluster_id strings). The problem says \"sorted selected IDs\". So we sort the selected IDs (alphabetically?).\n\nCheck specification: \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID. Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors. Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" Also earlier: \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision\". So we should include cluster_id and stability in rows.\n\nThus result format: {\"stability_rows\": [{\"cluster_id\": ..., \"stability\": ...}, ...], \"selected_ids\": [...]}\n\nWe must return that. The function must not mutate case. Use only standard library. Raise ValueError for invalid input.\n\nEdge cases: No retained clusters (all dropped) => stability_rows empty, selected_ids empty. If no clusters at all, maybe error? But valid input should have at least one cluster.\n\nAlso need to handle cluster_ids that appear in parent mapping but not in clusters list? Should raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Use a dict for clusters: cluster_id -> {size, birth, exits, parent, children list}\n- Build children from parent map: for each cluster_id, if parent is not None, add cluster_id to parent's children list. Also ensure parent exists.\n- Compute descendants via DFS or BFS for each cluster? Could compute descendants sets recursively with memoization. Since number of clusters is small, we can compute for each cluster the set of all descendant IDs (excluding itself). Use a function that traverses children recursively.\n- For ancestors: for a given cluster, we can follow parent chain and mark them blocked.\n\nWe must also consider that the parent mapping may define a forest (multiple roots). The problem doesn't say single root, but likely it's a tree. We'll handle any directed acyclic graph? It should be a tree (each node has at most one parent). We'll assume it's a tree.\n\nAlso need to ensure no cycles: we can detect cycles while building.\n\nNow, write the Python code as a string. Ensure it is properly escaped for JSON. Use triple quotes? No, we need to escape backslashes and quotes. We'll write the code in a normal string and then JSON-encode it.\n\nThe output must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and the value being the Python source string.\n\nWe'll produce the string.\n\nLet's write the solve function:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be a dict\")\n    required_keys = {'min_cluster_size', 'clusters', 'parent'}\n    if not required_keys.issubset(case.keys()):\n        raise ValueError(\"missing required keys\")\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\n    if not isinstance(min_size, int) or min_size < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid min_cluster_size\")\n    clusters_data = case['clusters']\n    parent_map = case['parent']\n    if not isinstance(clusters_data, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"clusters must be a list\")\n    # build cluster dict\n    clusters = {}\n    for cl in clusters_data:\n        if not isinstance(cl, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"cluster must be dict\")\n        cid = cl.get('cluster_id')\n        if not isinstance(cid, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"cluster_id missing or not string\")\n        if cid in clusters:\n            raise ValueError(f\"duplicate cluster_id {cid}\")\n        size = cl.get('size')\n        birth = cl.get('birth_lambda')\n        exits = cl.get('point_exit_lambdas')\n        if not isinstance(size, int) or size < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid size\")\n        if not isinstance(birth, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid birth_lambda\")\n        if not isinstance(exits, list) or not all(isinstance(x, (int, float)) for x in exits):\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid point_exit_lambdas\")\n        clusters[cid] = {'size': size, 'birth': birth, 'exits': exits, 'parent': None, 'children': []}\n    # add parent and children\n    for cid, pid in parent_map.items():\n        if cid not in clusters:\n            raise ValueError(f\"cluster_id {cid} in parent not in clusters\")\n        if pid is not None:\n            if pid not in clusters:\n                raise ValueError(f\"parent {pid} not in clusters\")\n            clusters[cid]['parent'] = pid\n            clusters[pid]['children'].append(cid)\n    # check for cycles using DFS\n    # not implemented for brevity but could be done; assume valid tree\n    # filter clusters by min_size\n    retained = {cid: info for cid, info in clusters.items() if info['size'] >= min_size}\n    # compute stability for each retained cluster\n    for cid, info in retained.items():\n        stability = sum(max(0, e - info['birth']) for e in info['exits'])\n        info['stability'] = stability\n    # sort retained clusters\n    sorted_clusters = sorted(retained.items(), key=lambda x: (-x[1]['stability'], x[0]))\n    # precompute all descendants for each cluster (strict descendants)\n    descendants = {}\n    def get_descendants(cid):\n        if cid in descendants:\n            return descendants[cid]\n        desc = set()\n        for child in clusters[cid]['children']:\n            desc.add(child)\n            desc.update(get_descendants(child))\n        descendants[cid] = desc\n        return desc\n    # compute for all clusters (including dropped? only needed for retained)\n    for cid in retained:\n        get_descendants(cid)\n    # selection\n    selected = set()\n    blocked = set()\n    for cid, info in sorted_clusters:\n        if cid in blocked:\n            continue\n        # check if any descendant is already selected\n        if any(d in selected for d in descendants[cid]):\n            continue\n        # select this cluster\n        selected.add(cid)\n        # block ancestors\n        p = clusters[cid]['parent']\n        while p is not None:\n            blocked.add(p)\n            p = clusters[p]['parent']\n    # construct result\n    # stability rows for all retained clusters, sorted by cluster_id\n    stability_rows = [{'cluster_id': cid, 'stability': retained[cid]['stability']} for cid in sorted(retained.keys())]\n    selected_ids = sorted(selected)  # sort lexicographically\n    return {'stability_rows': stability_rows, 'selected_ids': selected_ids}\n```\n\nBut wait: The stability for a cluster should be computed even if it's not retained? The problem says \"For each retained cluster sum\". So only retained clusters have stability computed. But then in ranking we only consider retained clusters. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to ensure that in the selection process, we only consider retained clusters. The ancestors we block should include all ancestors regardless of retention? The problem says \"then block its ancestors.\" Usually we block all ancestors, even if they are not retained (smaller than min_size). But if an ancestor is not retained, it won't be considered in selection anyway. But still we should block them to prevent any descendant that is under a non-retained cluster from causing issues? Actually if a cluster is not retained, it's not eligible for selection, so blocking is irrelevant. But for consistency, we can block all ancestors. However, note that descendants computation for a cluster includes all descendants, retained or not. So when checking if any descendant selected, we need to consider only selected ones (which are all retained, but if a non-retained cluster somehow got selected? No). So it's fine.\n\nPotential issue: The descendants set includes all descendants (including those not retained). But we only check selected set which contains retained clusters. So it's fine.\n\nAlso need to handle the case where a cluster has no parent (root). The parent pointer is None.\n\nNow, about the \"point_exit_lambdas\" - we are summing over all entries, even if they are negative? max(0, exit - birth). That's fine.\n\nNow, regarding validation: We need to check that the input structure matches the fixture schema. We'll do basic checks.\n\nAdd error for missing cluster_id, etc.\n\nAlso ensure that parent map keys are exactly the set of cluster_ids? Not necessarily; but we check each parent entry